we all know the s p 500 as one of the  safest stock market investments in the  world the index is regularly updated to  reflect the top 500 most profitable and  stable companies in america so you can  be pretty sure that as long as america  is still growing the s p 500 will be  growing as well but despite the  ubiquitous nature of the s p 500 most  people aren't aware of the company  behind the index sndp global aside from  deciding who stays and leave the s p 500  sntp global manages dozens of indices  with the focuses on various regions and  sectors such as the snp asia 50 and the  sntp midcap 400 snp also specializes in  providing companies and governments with  credit ratings in 2011 for example snp  downgraded the us government from aaa to  double a plus considering all these  background roles of snp i don't think  you'd be surprised that snp posing quite  a bit of money in fact they're the 153rd  most profitable company in the world  with a market cap right around 100  billion dollars so here's how snpp  global became the king of index funds  taking a look back the history of snpp  global circles back to a man named henry  varnam poor who was born on december 8  1812 in endo remain staying true to his  name henry's background was rather poor  henry and his brother john were the  first people in their family to go to  college and they both got a law degree  after graduation the two combined forces  and established a local law firm in  bangor maine this was a rather lucrative  career and the brothers made sure to  spend and invest the money as  effectively as possible for example in  the 1840s means rapidly growing timber  industry caught their attention they  invested heavily into the industry which  ended up making them quite wealthy and  now that they were wealthy the brothers  started to pursue what they truly wanted  for john this meant continuing to grow  his wealth by becoming a business tycoon  he went ahead and spent his time  expanding maine's real network and he  quickly became the business magnate that  he dreamed of as for henry on the other  hand he was mostly content with the  world that he had already made and he  had little desire to continue chasing  money so he decided to spend his time  trying to help other everyday investors  get to his level to do this henry took  up a job at one of his brother's many  companies called the american real world  journal and he became their manager and  editor in 1849  for the next 13 years henry regularly  published articles regarding the  financial and operational state future  of american railroad companies all this  work eventually culminated into a book  called history of railroads and canals  in the united states which made its  debut in 1860 as the name suggests the  book attempted to provide a detailed  account of the entire history of us  railroads and canals after publishing  this book henry felt that he had done  enough community service and decided to  follow his brother's footsteps deeper  into the business world in 1862 he took  on a role as a governor commissioner of  union pacific railroad and he was  promoted to secretary his very first  year but henry wasn't all that impressed  or excited about the job and would end  up leaving soon after henry returned to  spreading his knowledge about railroads  but this time he decided to join forces  with his son henry william poor together  they founded hv and hw pornco and in  1868 the company published two railroad  guide books which were updated on an  annual basis around this time henry's  brother john unfortunately passed away  at age 63 and this was a clear signal to  henry that he should start taking things  easy over the next decade henry slowly  stepped away from the company and let  his son take over completely  ironically though henry had many decades  left to go as he didn't end up passing  away until 1905 at age 92. so there was  no doubt that henry had lived a pretty  full life but his company porn the co  was just half of what would eventually  become s p global  moving into the 1900s we saw more people  enter the stock market and the creation  of new instruments called indicators  nowadays investors who solely rely on  indicators are often laughed at given  that the usefulness of indicators is  usually overstated nonetheless back in  the late 1800s and early 1900s stock  market indicators became all the crates  and amongst the plethora of indicators  that were created we saw one from a man  named charles dowd charles was a  journalist who had dedicated his entire  career to bringing transparency to  financial journalism he wanted to  demystify the stock market by making  financial news easily digestible every  day after markets closed charles put  together a two-page summary of the day's  financial news called the customer's  afternoon letter this newsletter became  so popular that in 1889 charles decided  to start his own publishing company you  might have heard of this one it's called  the wall street journal while the wall  street journal has become a stable  publishing company today no one really  associates it with charles something  that people do associate with charles  however is of course the dow jones  industrial average charles created the  stock market indicator in 1896 he wanted  to allow his readers to gage the health  of the stock market by just looking at  one number the dao has since become the  most cited stock market measure in  history unfortunately charles never  witnessed this as he passed away in 1902  but his invention would become the basis  of the second half of sntp global in  1906 we saw the creation of standard  statistics company which was heavily  inspired by pornco  instead of solely publishing about the  financial state of railroad companies  though standard posted about the  financial state of everything except for  the railroad industry given the lack of  financial news at the time this simple  alteration was quite successful  about 17 years later standard decided to  draw inspiration from another financial  icon which was of course charles doubt  standard couldn't just copy the dow  jones though they needed something to  make them unique so standard decided to  simply beat the dao in terms of quantity  instead of creating an index based on 30  companies standard decided to create an  indicator that encompassed 233 companies  and 26 industries surely this was a much  better indicator than the dow simple  statistics would tell you that the  greater the sample size the more  accurate the result over the next few  decades standard also created other  indices such as the 90 stock composite  price index which further grew the  popularity of the company by 1941 it  became clear that standard was here to  stay  so poor in the code decided to join  forces with them and together they  created standard and pores  over the next 16 years their indices  went to the moon as the stock market  roared after world war ii this garnered  the company's indicators more attention  than ever before and in 1957 they came  out with their crown jewel the s p 500.  standard and poor didn't stay standard  and poor for very long though in 1966  mcgraw-hill the company that tortured us  through high school and college went  ahead and purchased tender and poor they  kept the naming scheme of the s p 500  and other indices but they changed the  name of the company itself to reflect  the parent company mcgraw-hill despite  how popular snpp indices were they  didn't really make the company any money  at the time no investor or hedge fund  cared to invest in index funds index  funds were seen as garbage meant for  unsophisticated investors in fact  vanguard's founder john c bogo was  laughed at for trying to create a  company centered on index funds now this  doesn't mean that investors didn't pay  attention to the s 500 they paid very  close attention to the index because it  was a great indicator and the perfect  benchmark to beat in other words indices  were highly respected as an indicator  but not respected at all as an  investment considering this it made no  sense for mcgraw hill to create an index  fund based on their indices  instead they decided to leverage the  brand name of sndp and generate revenue  by providing other financial services  one of these endeavors was the custom  directory established in 1969 the  directory assigns each financial  instrument a special nine-digit number  that makes it much easier to clear and  settle traits today the directory  contains references to 57 million  financial instruments around the world  and it has become the standard for  clearing and settling trades mcgraw-hill  also spent significant effort in  expanding smp's credit rating system  which has become one of the most  respected credit rating systems in the  world mcgraw-hill also got sndp into the  market analysis sector for a fee sndp  will provide customers with a detailed  intelligence and analysis reports into  whichever financial market they choose  despite all the progress they were  making with snp macro hill was still an  education company at their core and they  spent the majority of their effort in  trying to grow the education side of the  company for example in 1986 mcgraw  bought out their largest education  competitor called the economy company  this acquisition made mcgraw the largest  education publisher in the us  all this growth with education put their  financial business on the back burner  but this would all change with the turn  of the century  ever since the early 2000s people  started to come to a realization while  index funds were boring and  unsophisticated they were also safe and  reliable and nothing made this clearer  than 90 of internet companies going  bankrupt but the pain wasn't just  limited to speculative internet startups  even the biggest most established tech  companies struggled to return to their  dot-com peaks and some of them like  cisco and intel still haven't beaten  their dot-com peaks considering this  investors became more open to the idea  of index fund investing you might not  make massive returns but you can be sure  that after a recession or even a  depression the indices will post new  all-time highs people also started to  argue that passive investing was even  better than active investing one of the  biggest proponents of this argument was  of course warren buffett in 2007 warren  buffett placed a 1 million bet that  index funds would outperform hedge funds  in the next decade warren ended up  winning the bet and this was the final  green light for investors to pile into  index funds not only were index funds  safer and less stressful but they  literally posted better returns than  hedge funds and even warren buffett  himself now just because these guys  couldn't beat the market doesn't mean  that it's impossible to beat the market  many investors have been beating the  market for decades like jim simons but  this requires a combination of work risk  and luck that doesn't seem to be worth  it for most anyway all the new interest  surrounding index funds brought in more  revenue than ever before for mcgraw hill  in the meantime their education business  wasn't doing nearly as well  mcgraw had just spent several years  trying to grow an online study network  called greatguru.com but this never took  off so mcgraw hill decided to ditch  education and go all in on sndp in 2012  they sold their education division to  apollo global management for 2.5 billion  dollars in cash following the sale the  company changed their name to  mcgraw-hill financial and they used this  name for the next couple of years  but by 2016 it was clear that everyone  knew the company for the s p indices so  they changed their name to s to b global  since then sntp global has grown into a  massive conglomerate that profits 2 to  2.5 billion every quarter but the most  ironic part about the business is their  stock price their entire brand is  centered on the idea that index fund  investing is superior to active  investing yet their stock price has  crushed the s p 500 since the financial  crisis growing over two thousand percent  which is roughly four times the s p 500  maybe it'll settle down over time but  we'll just have to see  do you guys prefer to pick stocks or  just invest in the s p 500 comment that  down below  also drop a like if you're a hodler and  of course consider checking out our  international channels to watch our  videos in other languages and consider  subscribing to see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one
 

